
  My recommendation is [1mexplicit watchers with assignee and creator auto-subscribed[0m. It's the one model where
  notifications reduce to a single query — "who watches this task" — instead of a growing pile of special cases, and it
  subsumes the first two rather than conflicting with them. The cost is one table and two endpoints, which is small now
  and genuinely awkward to retrofit once notification-fanout logic is written against a narrower rule.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What makes a task one a user "cares about"? → Watchers (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: the stack, since this is greenfield and the choice is one of the expensive ones to undo.

  - [1mTypeScript (Node + SQLite)[0m — one language across the empty [38;5;153mindex.html[39m frontend and the server. Buys shared types for
    the task and event shapes, which matters when notifications later have to agree with the task layer on an event
    schema; costs a Node toolchain in a repo that currently has none.
  - [1mPython (FastAPI + SQLite)[0m — matches the toolchain your environment is already set up around (micromamba + uv, ruff,
    mypy). Buys a familiar setup and good schema validation via Pydantic; costs a language boundary between server and
    browser, so event shapes get defined twice.
  - [1mGo or similar single-binary[0m — buys trivial deployment; costs the most unfamiliar ground for the least benefit at
    this size.

  My recommendation is [1mTypeScript + SQLite[0m. The one thing this project is actually about is an event contract shared
  between a producer (tasks) and a consumer (notifications), and having both sides typed from one definition is worth
  more here than toolchain familiarity. SQLite over Postgres either way — at this size Postgres is operational overhead
  with no payoff, and the swap later is a connection-string-and-migrations job, not a rewrite.
